{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/698995-advice-on-gameplay-balance","id":698995,"title":"Advice on gameplay balance?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-10-12T15:01:25-07:00","id":698995,"title":"Advice on gameplay balance?","content":"New member here, just getting into GMing for the first time in years and years. I used to be a geek for the oWoD but haven't really dug into anything RPG related in about a decade. Recently I picked up VtR though with the intent of running a game for a group of experienced friends. I've got plenty and plenty of fodder for the setting and possible plots but intend to take a sandbox approach to the game: create a vibrant city full of hooks and give the players freedom to explore it as they see fit. I'm probably putting them off in the &quot;backwoods&quot; of the city with the vague goal of instituting a bit more law and order and essentially gentrifying it for the Invictus. As such, I'm trying to kind of &quot;gate off&quot; potential threats. I want the risks in their part of town to be challenging but surmountable, with things getting dicier if they decide to proceed into more established neighborhoods.<br /> <br /> I know a lot if gameplay is contingent on how players build their characters, but does anyone who has more experience with the game's crunch have hard-and-fast rules or general tips for scaling NPCs to starting level characters? The kind of advice that will make it easier to say &quot;Okay. The coterie can just beat down these ghouls&quot;, &quot;They have even odds convincing this Carthian to cede territory&quot;, or &quot;No way are they charming this Daeva&quot;? Should I just use starting level character generation as a baseline for threats with a 50/50 chance of success, or has someone figured out a more nuanced rule of thumb?<br /> <br /> Additionally, are there any rules in supplements of home-brewed rules for simplified NPC stats? I cut my teeth on Star Wars by FFG a few months back and I loved their streamlined system for groups of goons (if I remember correctly, oWoD had a similar system, but I didn't see it in the VtR core). It made it easy to whip up a threat on the fly or set aside generic stats for a random archetype they might come across.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-12T17:14:05-07:00","id":699219,"title":"","content":"For simplified fighting stats, definitely check out how combat works in 2nd Edition Requiem. 1st Edition is pretty much a slog through health levels and combat resources until the GM (appropriately) fiat's a morale shift on the NPCs. In 2nd edition, they have some codified rules on NPCs surrendering to the PC's intents when they start taking lethal damage or damage over Stamina. Goon fighting gets a lot quicker in those cases, in my experience.<br /> <br /> For general NPC stat guidelines - I really recommend some kiddie pool missions to start out, which it sounds like you are doing with the 'backwoods' start. Give them lower-consequence conflicts to cut their teeth on. Not only does this let them explore their powers and the new system, but it lets you play with difficulty numbers and stats for NPCs where it doesn't mean life and death.<br /> <br /> An easy hack is also to follow the old adage, &quot;Pick your battlefield.&quot; - Start off the PCs in the action in an antagonists preferred battlefield. A social guy at a party, a bruiser guy in a street fight, a mental guy in a debate or research race. Give the antagonist a good pool (8-9) if it's contested, or make target successes = 4 if not. Let the players throw themselves at that, see how they do - but keep the consequences light for if they fail - and give them an out to changing the battlefield without much consequence if they hit the wall. Beat up the nerd, outsmart the succubus, etc.<br /> <br /> For ad hoc Requiem stats, I tend to set easy challenges at 1 success / or vs. a 4 pool, medium at 2-3 succ / vs. 6 pool, and hard at 4 succ / 8 pool. If a third trait is used (like disciplines) - bump the pool by +1, +2, +3 or 1 success.I don't let goons spend Willpower. I let Lieutenants/Mid-Range NPCs spend Willpower in life and death situations, and main antagonists can spend willpower just like a PC. <br /> <br /> This is all assuming people have most skills at 3 or lower. One thing I like to do is work a clause into the social contract which is basically: When any PC takes a Dot 4 skill or discipline, difficulties increase. Same again when someone takes a Dot 5. This still lets players buy whatever they want, but then they are also deciding as a group when they want stakes / difficulties to increase across the board.<br /> <br /> Now - this is all tabletop advice. If you happen to be doing a LARP, then I just recommend statting out all your NPCs, and coming up with some stock goon templates to use, and running them straight.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4047-holy","name":"Holy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4047_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"274"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-12T17:22:31-07:00","id":699225,"title":"","content":"That's exactly the sort of information I needed. Thanks!<br /> <br /> I actually am using the 2E core, and it seems very clean and streamlined, and yeah, it is TT.<br /> <br /> I was considering homebrewing some simple rules for faceless to mid-level opponents. I figured maybe give them a base dice pool for all rolls and then essentially stack them with specialty rules. So a gangster ghoul might get a base pool of 5 for physical, 4 for mental, and 2 for social and a +2 bonus for firearms, +1 for brawling, and +1 for intimidation. So his dice pool is 7 for actions in a gun fight, 6 in a fistfight, and 5 around the board for any other physical action he might take. Has anyone tried anything like this?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-12T18:30:08-07:00","id":699260,"title":"","content":"If you want some really simple NPC rules (esp. for mooks) check out the Retainer background.<br /> <br /> One thing to consider though, is that the nWOD systems drastically favor the side with bigger numbers. Five weak looking mooks, can be very dangerous to one PC. It takes a pretty big gap to expect to come away from a fight against multiple opponents in combat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-12T19:03:51-07:00","id":699266,"title":"","content":"This may not be the sort of thing you want, but here's how I've started running it after a number of years running WoD:<br /> <br /> I used to try and balance encounters to the PCs, like you might in many other systems. These days, though, I just give NPCs and monsters whatever stats I feel they should have (and don't bother statting anything that doesn't come up), and I let my PCs know they will have to use some judgement when it comes to Fight or Flight. Generally, this leads to a lot of cakewalks. Neonates aren't likely to be encountering other vampires as combat antagonists except maybe at the climax of a story, at least in my games, so most people they fight end up being kind of easy. That's fine. Vampires should have a lot of their encounters with humans be pretty easy. &quot;Boss fights&quot; are trickier. The number one concern in nWoD 1e or 2e is always going to be numbers. The side that is outnumbered has a huge disadvantage, so don't give your bosses too many minions, if you're the sort of have boss encounters. Numbers almost always trumps anything else, though you can generally outnumber with humans a little and still be okay.<br /> <br /> In the end, I have had my best success just statting characters as makes sense. If the Daeva seems like they've been at this a while, give them some 4s or even 5s in their social stats. Primogen of the Gangrel? Ought to have a pretty high Blood Potency and a low Humanity. That sort of thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1692-maina","name":"Maina","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1692_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"724"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-12T19:32:54-07:00","id":699305,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/698995-advice-on-gameplay-balance?p=699260#post699260\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you want some really simple NPC rules (esp. for mooks) check out the Retainer background.<br /> <br /> One thing to consider though, is that the nWOD systems drastically favor the side with bigger numbers. Five weak looking mooks, can be very dangerous to one PC. It takes a pretty big gap to expect to come away from a fight against multiple opponents in combat.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, using retainer is a clever idea actually.<br /> <br /> I considered using Fantasy Flight's system as inspiration for mook groups. They all share a health pool, have a flat attribute and essentially a specialty or two that affords one extra dice per mook, but they don't each roll. So if you have four thugs with melee specialty and 3 strength. They get a seven dice pool. I'd maybe let them split the pool for an extra action, but I think that might be dangerous given the combat mechanics.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maina</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/698995-advice-on-gameplay-balance?p=699266#post699266\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This may not be the sort of thing you want, but here's how I've started running it after a number of years running WoD:<br /> <br /> I used to try and balance encounters to the PCs, like you might in many other systems. These days, though, I just give NPCs and monsters whatever stats I feel they should have (and don't bother statting anything that doesn't come up), and I let my PCs know they will have to use some judgement when it comes to Fight or Flight. Generally, this leads to a lot of cakewalks. Neonates aren't likely to be encountering other vampires as combat antagonists except maybe at the climax of a story, at least in my games, so most people they fight end up being kind of easy. That's fine. Vampires should have a lot of their encounters with humans be pretty easy. &quot;Boss fights&quot; are trickier. The number one concern in nWoD 1e or 2e is always going to be numbers. The side that is outnumbered has a huge disadvantage, so don't give your bosses too many minions, if you're the sort of have boss encounters. Numbers almost always trumps anything else, though you can generally outnumber with humans a little and still be okay.<br /> <br /> In the end, I have had my best success just statting characters as makes sense. If the Daeva seems like they've been at this a while, give them some 4s or even 5s in their social stats. Primogen of the Gangrel? Ought to have a pretty high Blood Potency and a low Humanity. That sort of thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not planning on running a combat heavy game and didn't have combat rules particularly in mind. I'm probably overthinking it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-12T22:55:54-07:00","id":699516,"title":"","content":"The Retainer rules make great quick stats for individual NPCs and the Staff rules make for a decent abstraction of small groups. As for combat balance, I agree with Mania. As long as you make sure you leave the players options for non-combat solutions to problems, you don't need to worry too much about creating balanced encounters. I've always found unbalanced games to be more fun anyway. When everything is carefully designed to present a moderate challenge and curved to scale in difficulty as you gain Experience, it ends up feeling very artificial and game-y. Better if you always have to carefully consider the risks before using violence as a solution, because you know some NPCs are just going to be straight up too powerful for you to challenge physically.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-12T23:21:04-07:00","id":699521,"title":"","content":"Y'all may be right. My general intent isn't to build linear encounters but instead give the players a big sandbox to play in, but I figured I'd settle their territory down near scaled threats to get them situated. That may not be necessary. These players are pros.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-13T00:30:08-07:00","id":699541,"title":"","content":"Another thought when it comes to mooks and combat:<br /> <br /> Take a good look at the Down &amp; Dirty mechanics, instead of the full combat mechanics. As long as the NPCs do not have the intent to kill the PCs, it's a very good way to ensure there's no TPK from a fight that's too imbalanced from things that are harder to gauge (as I mentioned before, numbers can be a big deal in this). While it can be less fun if you have a bunch of combat focused PCs, if you're running a combat-light game, allowing the PCs to use the teamwork rules with your combat heavy as the primary in a Down &amp; Dirty vs the &quot;mooks&quot; with whatever dice-pool you want to give them, lets you move through a narratively minor fight quickly, and even if the PCs roll like crap, they only take an amount of damage based on how well the mooks roll (where the PCs can have the intent to kill and just wipe them all out).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}